## Proof

**Step 1: Simplify the product.**

The product is
$$\prod_{i=1}^{10}\binom{20-(a_1+\cdots+a_{i-1})}{a_i}.$$

Writing $S_{i-1}=a_1+\cdots+a_{i-1}$ with $S_0=0$, this telescopes as the multinomial coefficient:
$$\prod_{i=1}^{10}\binom{20-S_{i-1}}{a_i}=\frac{20!}{(20-S_1)!\cdot(20-S_1)!/...}=\frac{20!}{a_1!\,a_2!\cdots a_{10}!}=\binom{20}{a_1,\ldots,a_{10}}.$$

So
$$f(a_1,\ldots,a_{10})=\binom{20}{a_1,\ldots,a_{10}}\sum_{i=1}^{10}c_i\,a_i,\qquad c_i=\binom{18+i}{19}.$$

**Step 2: Count the tuples.**

The number of nonnegative integer 10-tuples with $\sum a_i=20$ is $\binom{20+10-1}{10-1}=\binom{29}{9}$.

**Step 3: Compute $\sum f$ over all tuples.**

By linearity,
$$\sum_{\sum a_i=20}f=\sum_{i=1}^{10}c_i\Bigl(\sum_{\sum a_i=20}\binom{20}{a_1,\ldots,a_{10}}\,a_i\Bigr).$$

Using the multinomial theorem $(x_1+\cdots+x_{10})^{20}=\sum\binom{20}{a}x_1^{a_1}\cdots x_{10}^{a_{10}}$ and differentiating with respect to $x_i$, then setting all $x_j=1$:
$$\sum_{\sum a_i=20}\binom{20}{a_1,\ldots,a_{10}}\,a_i = \left.\frac{\partial}{\partial x_i}(x_1+\cdots+x_{10})^{20}\right|_{x_j=1}=20.$$

Hence
$$\sum f = 20\sum_{i=1}^{10}\binom{18+i}{19}=20\sum_{k=19}^{28}\binom{k}{19}.$$

By the hockey-stick identity,
$$\sum_{k=19}^{28}\binom{k}{19}=\binom{29}{20}=\binom{29}{9}.$$

Therefore
$$\sum f = 20\binom{29}{9}.$$

**Step 4: Compute $N$.**

$$N=\frac{\sum f}{\binom{29}{9}}=\frac{20\binom{29}{9}}{\binom{29}{9}}=20.$$

**Step 5: Divisors of $N=20$.**

$20=2^2\cdot 5$, so the number of positive divisors is $(2+1)(1+1)=6$.

